course: dictionary
lesson: 12331
title: Coupons and money off
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A coupon is a piece of paper or a code that helps you pay less when you shop. «Mum used a coupon to get money off her shopping.» The words money off tell you a coupon is a kind of bargain ticket.
explain: Coupons can be printed in a newspaper or magazine, or sent as a code on a phone or computer. «Ravi cut out the coupon from the magazine and took it to the shop.» Cut out is a clue: a paper coupon is something you can cut out and keep.
explain: In maths, coupons give discounts. «The shoes cost £40, and the coupon gave 20% off.» To find the new price, work out 20% of £40, which is £8, then take it away from £40 to get £32. Always check that your answer makes sense: the new price should be lower than the old one.
explain: Be careful online. «A pop-up said, Free coupon: type your bank card number here!» A coupon that asks for your bank details is a warning sign. Stop, and ask a grown-up before you click or type anything.
fact: A coupon is a piece of paper or a code that gets you money off, or something free, when you buy.
fact: A coupon can also be a form cut from a newspaper or magazine, used to order or enter something.
fact: «Buy one get one free» is a coupon offer: you pay for one item and the second one is free.
fact: «20% off» means you pay 20% less than the normal price.
fact: Fake coupons online can be scams, so ask a grown-up before you click or give any details.
exercise: «Mum used a coupon to get money off her shopping.» What is a coupon? | A loud beep made by the till | A shop that sells only sweets | *A piece of paper or a code that gets you money off or something free when you buy | A bag you carry shopping home in
exercise: «The shoes cost £40, and the coupon gave 20% off.» What is the new price? | *£32 | £8 | £20 | £40
exercise: A pop-up asks for your bank password to claim a free coupon. What should you do? | Share it with all your friends | *Stop, and ask a grown-up before you go on | Pay for the coupon with your bank card | Type in your password quickly so you do not miss out
test: «Ravi cut out the coupon from the magazine.» Where did the coupon come from? | *A newspaper or magazine | A tree in the park | A bus ticket | A phone shop
test: «Buy one get one free» on a £6 drink. How much do you pay for two drinks? | £12 | £0 | *£6 | £3
exam: «A coupon says 10% off a £50 jacket.» How much do you pay for the jacket? | £5 | *£45 | £50 | £40
